  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "The div-MEX of an array is defined to be the smallest positive integer that doesn't divide any element of the array.\n\nFor example, the div-MEX of [4,10,1,9,21][4, 10, 1, 9, 21][4,10,1,9,21] is 666, because every integer from 111 to 555 divides at least one element of the array, but 666 doesn't divide any of them.\n\nYou're given an array AAA of length NNN, and an integer MMM. The elements of AAA all lie between 111 and MMM.\n\n**At most once**, you can choose an index iii (1≤i≤N1 \\leq i \\leq N1≤i≤N) and an integer xxx (1≤x≤M1 \\leq x \\leq M1≤x≤M), and replace AiA_iAi​ with xxx.\n\nFind the maximum possible div-MEX of the final array AAA.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and MMM — the length of the array and the maximum allowed value, respectively.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​ — the elements of array AAA.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the maximum possible div-MEX of array AAA after making at most one replacement.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤2⋅1051 \\leq T \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤T≤2⋅105",
      "1≤N,M≤5⋅1051 \\leq N, M \\leq 5\\cdot 10^51≤N,M≤5⋅105",
      "1≤Ai≤M1 \\leq A_i \\leq M1≤Ai​≤M",
      "The sum of NNN and the sum of MMM over all test cases both won't exceed 5⋅1055\\cdot 10^55⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n4 1\n1 1 1 1\n5 5\n1 3 4 2 2\n4 5\n2 1 5 2\n7 20\n15 20 13 2 17 20 12",
        "output": "2\n6\n4\n8",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Since M=1M = 1M=1, all the elements of AAA must be 111 no matter what.\nThe div-MEX is thus 222.\nTest case 222: Replace A4=2A_4 = 2A4​=2 with 555, to obtain the array [1,3,4,5,2][1, 3, 4, 5, 2][1,3,4,5,2].\nEvery number from 111 to 555 is present in AAA, while 666 doesn't divide anything in AAA.\nTest case 333: Replace A2=1A_2 = 1A2​=1 with 333, to obtain [2,3,5,2][2, 3, 5, 2][2,3,5,2].\n1,2,31, 2, 31,2,3 all have multiples in the array, but 444 does not.\nThis is optimal."
      }
    ]
  },
